<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    ServiceNow Update Set record-definitions for the conditional
    polymorphic field rendering on the `x_casemgmt_case_party` custom
    table inside the scoped application `x_casemgmt`.

    This file produces FOUR records - two `sys_ui_policy` rows and their
    two `sys_ui_policy_action` children - which together implement AAP
    Section 0.4.3 ("Polymorphic association via single-table choice +
    UI Policy") and the `ui_policy/` entry of AAP Section 0.4.1.

    PURPOSE (AAP Section 0.4.3):
      Implement the ServiceNow-native equivalent of ArkCase's JPA
      single-table inheritance pattern from PersonAssociation.java, which
      uses:
          @Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.SINGLE_TABLE)
          @DiscriminatorColumn(name = "cm_class_name",
                               discriminatorType = DiscriminatorType.STRING)
          @DiscriminatorValue("com.armedia.acm.plugins.person.model.PersonAssociation")
      collapsed into a single ServiceNow table `x_casemgmt_case_party`
      with a `party_type` Choice field that conditionally shows/hides
      and conditionally requires the `person` (Reference -> sys_user)
      and `organization` (Reference -> core_company) reference fields.

    BEHAVIOR MATRIX (the two reversible policies produce exactly these
    three states, on form load AND on every change of `party_type`):

        Case 1: party_type == "Person"
            Person policy condition TRUE  -> person       : visible, mandatory
            Organization policy FALSE     -> organization : hidden, not mandatory

        Case 2: party_type == "Organization"
            Person policy condition FALSE -> person       : hidden, not mandatory
            Organization policy TRUE      -> organization : visible, mandatory

        Case 3: party_type == "" (empty, new record)
            Both conditions FALSE         -> person       : hidden, not mandatory
                                          -> organization : hidden, not mandatory

    ARCHITECTURE DECISION - WHY DECLARATIVE, AND WHY ONE FIELD PER POLICY:

      An earlier revision of this file shipped ONE policy with
      `run_scripts=true`, an imperative `onCondition()` script, ZERO
      `sys_ui_policy_action` children, and the condition string
      `party_typeISNOTEMPTY^ORparty_typeISEMPTY^EQ`.

      That condition is a tautology with a trailing malformed `^EQ`
      separator: it is true for every possible value of `party_type`,
      so the policy carried no meaningful discriminator predicate. It
      was measured on the running instance to apply the correct state at
      form LOAD and then never re-apply it when `party_type` changed, so
      a user could not complete a polymorphic party through the form UI -
      picking "Person" on a new record left the `person` field hidden.

      The fix is declarative, and the key design point is that EACH
      POLICY OWNS EXACTLY ONE FIELD:

        - "Case Party Conditional Fields - Person" watches
          `party_type=Person` and acts ONLY on `person`.
        - "Case Party Conditional Fields - Organization" watches
          `party_type=Organization` and acts ONLY on `organization`.

      Because no field is written by more than one policy, the two
      policies can never contend for the same field and the evaluation
      ORDER between them is irrelevant. That is what makes the
      empty-state case deterministic: both conditions are false, both
      policies reverse, and each reverses its own single field to
      hidden + not mandatory. (The order-conflict hazard that the
      previous revision documented applies only to the different design
      where both policies act on BOTH fields; this file does not do
      that.)

      `reverse_if_false=true` on both policies is what supplies the
      "on change" behaviour the previous revision lacked: the platform
      registers `party_type` as a watched field from the condition
      itself, re-evaluates on every change, and applies either the
      forward actions or their reverse. `on_load=true` additionally
      applies the correct state when an existing party record is
      opened.

      `run_scripts` is FALSE on both policies and both script bodies are
      empty: there is no imperative logic left to maintain, and no dead
      script is carried. Field values are never cleared (`cleared=false`
      on both actions), so toggling `party_type` back and forth never
      destroys data the user already entered.

    REQUIRED FIELDS HANDLED BY POLICY (NOT at dictionary level):
      Per the parent dictionary entries
        ../dictionary/x_casemgmt_case_party_person.xml
        ../dictionary/x_casemgmt_case_party_organization.xml
      the dictionary-level `<mandatory>false</mandatory>` is INTENTIONAL.
      The conditional mandatory state is enforced HERE by the
      `mandatory=true` action plus its reverse. This is the canonical
      ServiceNow pattern for polymorphic mandatory: the dictionary
      declares the column as nullable, and the UI Policy enforces
      conditional required-ness based on the discriminator value. That
      design is documented in those dictionary entries' `<comments>`
      element.

    SOURCE-SIDE SEMANTIC REFERENCE (read-only context, NOT byte-for-byte
    ported per AAP Section 0.2.1.3):

      acm-plugins/acm-default-plugins/acm-person-plugin/src/main/java/
      com/armedia/acm/plugins/person/model/PersonAssociation.java
        line 68-72:
          @Entity
          @Table(name = "acm_person_assoc")
          @Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.SINGLE_TABLE)
          @DiscriminatorColumn(name = "cm_class_name",
                               discriminatorType = DiscriminatorType.STRING)
          @DiscriminatorValue("com.armedia.acm.plugins.person.model.PersonAssociation")
        line 86-88:
          @ManyToOne(cascade = { ... }, optional = false)
          @JoinColumn(name = "cm_person_assoc_person_id", nullable = false)
          private Person person;

      acm-plugins/acm-default-plugins/acm-person-plugin/src/main/java/
      com/armedia/acm/plugins/person/model/PersonOrganizationAssociation.java
        line 64-69:
          @Entity
          @Table(name = "acm_person_org_assoc")
          @Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.SINGLE_TABLE)
          @DiscriminatorColumn(name = "cm_class_name", ...)
          @DiscriminatorValue("com.armedia.acm.plugins.person.model.PersonOrganizationAssociation")
        line 82-88:
          @ManyToOne(cascade = { ... }, optional = false)
          @JoinColumn(name = "cm_person_id", nullable = false)
          private Person person;
          @ManyToOne(cascade = { ... }, optional = false)
          @JoinColumn(name = "cm_organization_id", nullable = false)
          private Organization organization;

      ArkCase's polymorphism uses two separate JPA entities living in
      two separate physical tables (`acm_person_assoc` and
      `acm_person_org_assoc`), each with its own mandatory @ManyToOne
      reference. The discriminator column stores the Java fully-qualified
      class name - opaque to non-Java consumers, fragile under refactors.

      The ServiceNow target FIXES this by collapsing both ArkCase
      tables into ONE table (x_casemgmt_case_party) with:
        - A user-friendly `party_type` Choice field with values
          "Person" and "Organization" (per
          ../choices/sys_choice_case_party_party_type.xml) replacing
          the JVM class-name discriminator
        - A `person` reference field (Reference -> sys_user) replacing
          the @ManyToOne Person reference
        - An `organization` reference field (Reference -> core_company)
          replacing the @ManyToOne Organization reference
        - A `role_label` String(100) field replacing the legacy
          `personType`/`personToOrganizationAssociationType` Strings

      THESE UI POLICIES are the ONLY mechanism that enforces the
      polymorphism at the form layer. Without them the form would always
      show BOTH `person` and `organization` reference fields, which would
      confuse users and allow inconsistent records (e.g. a row with
      party_type=Person but `organization` populated).

    CONDITION STRINGS:
      Person policy       : party_type=Person
      Organization policy : party_type=Organization
      Both are single-term encoded queries against the discriminator
      field. The right-hand literals MUST match the `<value>` of the
      corresponding records in
      ../choices/sys_choice_case_party_party_type.xml byte-for-byte.

    XML CONVENTIONS HONORED (matching sibling files in this app):
      - Wrapper element: `<unload unload_date="2025-01-01 00:00:00">`
        matching the sibling pattern used by `../choices/sys_choice_*.xml`.
      - Element ordering: alphabetical within each record.
      - Only columns that actually exist on the target tables are
        emitted. `sys_ui_policy` has no `name` column (its `sys_name` is
        derived from `short_description`), so no `<name>` element is
        written; `sys_ui_policy_action` has no `sys_domain` or
        `sys_overrides` column, so neither is written on the action rows.
      - `<sys_scope>` carries the scoped application's sys_id with a
        `display_value` attribute, which is the convention every other
        record in this package uses (see ../portal/sp_portal_*.xml,
        ../portal/rest/sys_ws_operation_*.xml).
      - `<ui_policy>` on each action carries its parent policy's sys_id
        with a `display_value` attribute. Both parents are defined in
        THIS file and are carried in the same Update Set, and the
        preview resolver matches an intra-set reference through the
        target block's canonical `<table>_<sys_id>` update name, so the
        reference previews clean on a fresh instance.
      - sys_id values:
          854295ebb4c8309eea6a52aa788ec8a9  Person policy - a pinned
            constant carried forward unchanged from the previous
            revision of this file, so that the already-deployed row and
            its Update Set block keep their identity. It is a fixed
            literal in this file, not a hash of any string.
          507da6cb683691d5a09285ab09297b22  Organization policy
            = md5("x_casemgmt_case_party.ui_policy.conditional_fields.organization")
          3ec2d72d2b69e11d331037a908ea9fa4  Person action
            = md5("x_casemgmt_case_party.ui_policy_action.person")
          4f52dd6c0ce634ba474075b15f28ce39  Organization action
            = md5("x_casemgmt_case_party.ui_policy_action.organization")
        The three hashes are recomputable, e.g.
          printf '%s' 'x_casemgmt_case_party.ui_policy_action.person' \
              | md5sum | cut -c1-32
        so re-generating this file is byte-deterministic.
      - No PII: these records carry structural state only.

    CROSS-REFERENCES (AAP Section 0.5.2 dependency ordering):
      THESE RECORDS MUST LOAD AFTER:
        - ../app/sys_app/x_casemgmt_case_management.xml (the scoped
          application record, against which <sys_scope>/<sys_package>
          resolve)
        - ../tables/x_casemgmt_case_party.xml (parent table; both
          policies and both actions target this table by name)
        - ../dictionary/x_casemgmt_case_party_party_type.xml (the
          discriminator field named by both conditions)
        - ../dictionary/x_casemgmt_case_party_person.xml (the field the
          Person action operates on)
        - ../dictionary/x_casemgmt_case_party_organization.xml (the
          field the Organization action operates on)
        - ../choices/sys_choice_case_party_party_type.xml (supplies the
          'Person' and 'Organization' condition literals)
      Within this file, each `sys_ui_policy` MUST load before the
      `sys_ui_policy_action` that references it; the records are written
      in that order.

      THESE RECORDS MUST LOAD BEFORE:
        - ../seed-data/parties/x_casemgmt_case_party_demo_*.xml (so the
          synthetic demo parties render with the correct field-visibility
          state when opened in the form post-import)

    AAP COMPLIANCE SUMMARY:
      - Scoped-namespace exclusivity (AAP 0.7.2): `<sys_scope>`,
        `<sys_package>` and `<table>` all reference the scoped app and
        its `x_casemgmt_*` table.
      - No email notifications (AAP 0.7.2): no notification artefacts.
      - Single Update Set deliverable (AAP 0.7.2): these four records
        are carried as four blocks of the exported Update Set.
      - Polymorphic UI Policy per AAP 0.4.3: `party_type` drives which
        reference field is shown and required.
      - Replicates ArkCase's party-association model (AAP 0.1.1): the
        Person/Organization branches replace ArkCase's two JPA entity
        classes (PersonAssociation, PersonOrganizationAssociation).
      - Applies on load AND on change, so a party can be completed and
        saved entirely through the form UI.
-->
<unload unload_date="2025-01-01 00:00:00">
<sys_ui_policy action="INSERT_OR_UPDATE">
